Additional inverse control — uncovered target-directory alias:
  Setup on a fresh fixture on macOS and Linux:
    mkdir -p target/debug/build/r46-generated/out
    printf '%s\n' '// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT' 'fn generated() {}' > target/debug/build/r46-generated/out/generated.rs
    ln -s ../target/debug/build/r46-generated/out src/generated-target
    CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$PWD/target" bash ../acceptance-v5.sh
  Expected from review/DECISION.md's definition: exit 0, because generated.rs is physically under Cargo's target directory, which is excluded by identity and is stated to be unaffected by path spelling or symlinks.
  Observed on macOS BSD find: exit 1; header check counted 6 files and reported src/generated-target/generated.rs:1:(a) and :1:(f).
  Observed on Linux GNU find: exit 1; header check counted 6 files and reported src/generated-target/generated.rs:1:(a) and :1:(f).
  Cause: -samefile "$r46_target" prunes only the target root. With find -L, a symlink to a descendant directory of target is not the same file as target, so find traverses it and reaches generated Rust sources through the alias.

